Given, \[V_{C_1}(0^-)=20~\text{V},\qquad
V_{C_2}(0^-)=0~\text{V}\] Given circuit is shown below. <img src="img/7b5426149a8f6b10.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Transform domain: <img src="img/2cfd1ab73be36973.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Applying KVL, \[-\frac{20}{s}+\frac{1}{2s}I(s)+sI(s)+\frac{1}{2s}I(s)=0\] \[\frac{20}{s}
=
I(s)\left[\frac{1}{2s}+s+\frac{1}{2s}\right]\] \[I(s)
=
\frac{\frac{20}{s}}
{\frac{1}{2s}+\frac{1}{2s}+s}
=
\frac{20}{s^2+1}
\tag{i}\] From figure, \[-V_{C_1}(s)+\left(s+\frac{1}{2s}\right)I(s)=0\] \[V_{C_1}(s)
=
\left(s+\frac{1}{2s}\right)I(s)\] Using (i), \[V_{C_1}(s)
=
\left(s+\frac{1}{2s}\right)\frac{20}{s^2+1}
=
\frac{10s}{s^2+1}+\frac{10}{s}\] Taking inverse Laplace transform, \[V_{C_1}(t)=10(1+\cos t)\,u(t)
\tag{ii}\] Also, \[V_{C_2}(s)
=
I(s)\frac{1}{2s}
=
\frac{20}{s^2+1}\cdot\frac{1}{2s}
=
\frac{10}{s}-\frac{10s}{s^2+1}\] Taking inverse Laplace transform, \[V_{C_2}(t)=10(1-\cos t)\,u(t)
\tag{iii}\] From (ii) and (iii), \[\boxed{
\text{(A)}\
V_{C_1}(t)=10(1+\cos t)u(t),\quad
V_{C_2}(t)=10(1-\cos t)u(t)
}\]

Given circuit is shown below, <img src="img/90b39e15d263cdf0.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> <ol><li>\(V_R=20~\text{V}\) when \(R=10~\Omega\)</li><li>\(V_R=30~\text{V}\) when \(R=20~\Omega\)</li></ol> <strong>Thevenin's equivalent circuit :</strong><br/><img src="img/c04f0b370d0eed94.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> \[V_R=V_{TH}\times\frac{R}{R+R_{TH}}
\tag{i}\] From the given data, \[20=V_{TH}\times\frac{10}{10+R_{TH}}\] \[10V_{TH}-20R_{TH}=200
\tag{ii}\] Also, \[30=V_{TH}\times\frac{20}{20+R_{TH}}\] \[20V_{TH}-30R_{TH}=600
\tag{iii}\] From (ii) and (iii), \[V_{TH}=60~\text{V}\] \[R_{TH}=20~\Omega\] The equivalent circuit becomes, <img src="img/473bc7e38eecc411.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> From (i), \[V_R=60\times\frac{R}{R+20}\] When \(R=80~\Omega\), \[V_R=60\times\frac{80}{80+20}
=48~\text{V}\] (A) \(48~\text{V}\)

Given circuit is shown below, <img src="img/b77a8e999051956d.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> <strong>Thevenin's equivalent circuit :</strong><br/><img src="img/d353ff46f54254cd.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> For a resistive network, maximum power is transferred to \(R_L\) when \[R_L=R_{TH}
\tag{i}\] and \[P_{\max}=\frac{V_{TH}^{2}}{4R_{TH}}\] <strong>Calculation of \(V_{TH}\) :</strong><br/><img src="img/315a1d19f6549549.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> From the figure, \[-V_{TH}+10+(1)(6)=0\] \[V_{TH}=6\times1+10=16~\text{V}\] <strong>Calculation of \(R_{TH}\) :</strong><br/>(When dependent sources are not present) Replace all independent sources by their internal resistances, i.e., short circuit all independent voltage sources \((R_{in}=0)\) and open circuit all independent current sources \((R_{in}=\infty)\). <img src="img/c50f50c40a52cd1e.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> \[R_{TH}=R=1~\Omega\] <strong>Thevenin's equivalent circuit :</strong><br/><img src="img/121fc20fd40a0e8e.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Load current is, \[I_L=\frac{V_{TH}}{R_{TH}+R}
=\frac{16}{1+1}
=8~\text{A}\] \[P_{\max}=|I_L|^2R_L
=8^2\times1
=64~\text{W}\] or \[P_{\max}
=
\frac{V_{TH}^{2}}{4R_{TH}}
=
\frac{16^2}{4\times1}
=
64~\text{W}\] (C) \(64~\text{W}\)

For a sinusoidal output, \[\left(\frac{dV_{o}}{dt}\right)_{\max}=2\pi fV_{o(\text{peak})}\] Given: \[\text{Slew Rate}=1\,\frac{\text{V}}{\mu\text{s}}=10^{6}\,\frac{\text{V}}{\text{s}}\] The amplifier gain is \(+10\), and the input peak amplitude is \(1\,\text{V}\). Hence, \[V_{o(\text{peak})}=10\times1=10\,\text{V}\] For undistorted output, \[2\pi fV_{o(\text{peak})}\leq \text{SR}\] \[f_{\max}=\frac{\text{SR}}{2\pi V_{o(\text{peak})}}
=\frac{10^{6}}{2\pi\times10}
=1.5915\times10^{4}\,\text{Hz}\] \[f_{\max}\approx15.92\,\text{kHz}\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }15.92\,\text{kHz}}\]

From Q10.00.2, the overall open loop transfer function is: \[G(s) = K\left[\frac{-6s+20}{(s+2)(s+10)}\right]\] The characteristic equation is: \[1+G(s) = 0\] \[1+K\left[\frac{-6s+20}{(s+2)(s+10)}\right] = 0\] \[1+K\left[\frac{-6s+20}{(s+2)(s+10)}\right] = 0\] \[s^2+s(12-6K)+20(1+K) = 0\] For stability: \[12-6K &gt; 0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad 1+K&gt;0\] \[K &lt;2 \qquad  \text{and} \qquad K&gt;-1\] The closed-loop transfer function is \[T(s)=\frac{\dfrac{K(20-6s)}{(s+2)(s+10)}}
{1+\dfrac{K(20-6s)}{(s+2)(s+10)}}\] For steady state, substitute \(s=0\): \[T(0)
=
\frac{\dfrac{K(20)}{(2)(10)}}
{1+\dfrac{K(20)}{(2)(10)}}
=
\frac{\dfrac{20K}{20}}
{1+\dfrac{20K}{20}}
=
\frac{K}{1+K}\] Evaluating at the stability limits: <strong>For \(K=-1\)</strong><br/>\[T(0)=\frac{-1}{1-1}
\Rightarrow \infty\] The steady-state gain is unbounded. <strong>For \(K=2\)</strong><br/>\[T(0)=\frac{2}{1+2}
=\frac{2}{3}=0.67\] Hence, the maximum finite steady-state gain is: \[\boxed{0.67}\]
